\section{When to use it}{

This plot earns its keep when the grouping has several levels. With only two
groups it is a single column of cells and a comparison table says the same
thing more compactly. A cluster assignment is the typical case: the clusters
already exist, and the question is what actually distinguishes each one from
the reference cluster.

The result is one column per non-referent group and one row per measure, so
a block structure in the cells \emph{is} the phenotype definition - each group
standing apart on its own set of variables, and variables that separate
nothing left blank across the whole row.

Every cell is backed by an auditable row in \code{Results}: the contrast, the
model that produced it, the group sizes, the raw and adjusted p-values, and
the correction that was applied.
